\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Declarative Knowledge:}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Curvature:} The curvature of a curve at a point in two-dimensional space is the rate at which the curve deviates from being a straight line near that point. For a curve parameterized by arc length, the curvature is the magnitude of the derivative of the unit tangent vector with respect to arc length.
    \item \textbf{Closed Convex Curve:} A closed convex curve in the Euclidean plane signifies a curve that starts and ends at the same point, enclosing a region, where any line segment joining two points on the curve remains inside or on the curve boundary.
    \item \textbf{Average Curvature Theorem:} Gauss-Bonnet theorem implies that for a simple closed curve in the Euclidean plane, the integral of curvature over the curve is $2\pi$. This results in the average curvature being $2\pi/L$, where $L$ is the length of the curve.
  \end{itemize}

  \item \textbf{Procedural Knowledge:}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Compute Curvature:} For a closed curve, find its arc length parameterization. Calculate the curvature by differentiating the unit tangent vector with respect to arc length.
    \item \textbf{Integrate to Find Total Curvature:} Use the Gaussian curvature integral relationship for a closed curve to find the total curvature, which for closed convex curves is $2\pi$ due to the Gauss-Bonnet theorem.
    \item \textbf{Average Curvature Calculation:} Divide the total curvature by the total length $L$ to determine the average curvature of the closed convex curve.
  \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

